(2) The implementation of the electronic-based Gender and Child Data System of Padang Sidempuan City includes the sending, verification, inputting and publication of data.

(3) Utilization of the Gender and Child Data System of Padang Sidempuan City consists of:

a. regional apparatus within the Padang Sidempuan City Government;
b. agencies domiciled in Padang Sidempuan City;
c. the general public/public.

Article 24

The PUG Working Group as referred to in article 23 has the following tasks:

a. promoting and facilitating PUG to each SKPD;
b. conducting socialization and advocacy of PUG to Sub-district Heads, Village Heads, and Urban Village Heads;
c. preparing work programs every year;
d. encouraging the realization of gender-responsive planning and budgeting;
e. preparing the work plan of the PUG Working Group every year;
f. being accountable to the Mayor through the Deputy Mayor;
g. formulating policy recommendations to the Mayor;
h. preparing Gender Profiles;
i. monitoring the implementation of PUG in each agency;
j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of regional budgets;
k. preparing the Regional Action Plan (RANDA) for PUG and;
1. encouraging the selection and appointment of Focal Points in each SKPD.

Article 25

1. The PUG Focal Point in each SKPD consists of officials and/or staff in charge of planning and/or programs.

2021, No. 3 -87­

2. The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), has the following tasks:

a. promoting gender mainstreaming in the work unit;
b. facilitating the preparation of work plans and Gender-responsive SKPD budgeting;
c. conducting training, socialization, advocacy of gender mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the SKPD;
d. encouraging the implementation of gender analysis on policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; and
e. facilitating the preparation of gender data in each SKPD;
3. The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), is selected and appointed by the head/leader of the SKPD.

Article 31

1. In the context of handling family psychosocial problems, recovery of family psychosocial conditions and strengthening family resilience, a Family Welfare Consultation Institution shall be established in the region.
2. With regard to the Family Welfare Consultation Institution, the Mayor has the following authorities as follows:
a. implementing policies for the operation of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;
b. implementing norms, standards, procedures, and criteria regarding the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;
c. implementing service standards of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;
d. implementing and managing data of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;

2021, No. 3 -89­
e. implementing capacity strengthening of human resources and institutions of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;
f. providing facilities and infrastructure for services of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution;
g. implementing family welfare cooperation;
h. implementing coordination with relevant agencies, business sector and the community; and/or
1. implementing monitoring and evaluation.

Article 32

1. Services of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution shall be organized by prioritizing strategies to build networks and partnerships.
2. Types of services of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution consist of:
a. consultation;
b. counseling;
c. information;
d. advocacy;
e. referral; and
f. outreach.
3. Networks and partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. potential and sources of social welfare;
b. Hospitals;
c. Indonesian National Police;
d. Bureau/Institution/Legal Aid Post;
e. Trauma Center Shelter;
f. Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment;
g. other public services that can serve as networks and partnerships of the Family Welfare Consultation Institution.
4. Networks and partnerships as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be conducted through:
a. cooperation;
b. coordination; and/or
c. collaboration;
5. Further provisions regarding the Family Welfare Consultation Institution shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 38

The development of a Child-Friendly City as referred to in Article 37 shall be implemented based on the following principles:

a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information disclosure, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not distinguishing ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;
c. best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;
d. right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; and

2021, No. 3 -91­
e. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion, is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters affecting them.

Article 39

The development of a Child-Friendly City is directed at the fulfillment of children's rights which include:

a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time utilization, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.

Article 40

1. In order to realize a Child-Friendly City, stages of Child-Friendly City development shall be prepared which include:
a. preparation;
b. planning;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.
2. In every stage of Child-Friendly City development, the views of children obtained through child consultation must be considered.

Article 42

1. The main tasks of the Child-Friendly City Task Force are as follows:
a. coordinating various efforts for the development of a Child-Friendly City;
b. preparing a Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly City Development;
c. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication for the development of a Child-Friendly City;
d. monitoring the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly City Development;
e. conducting evaluation at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly City Development; and
f. submitting reports to the Mayor.
2. Further provisions regarding the Child-Friendly City Task Force shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

